---
components: ["IcicleChart"]
description: Icicle chart shows a hierarchy as nested slices, each level a row and each node as wide as its share, for bundles, budgets, and file trees.
---

<script>
  import { IcicleChart } from "carbon-components-svelte/viz";

  const bundle = [
    { id: "bundle", parent: null, name: "bundle" },
    { id: "src", parent: "bundle", name: "src" },
    { id: "viz", parent: "src", name: "viz" },
    { id: "charts", parent: "viz", name: "charts", bytes: 210 },
    { id: "micro", parent: "viz", name: "micrographics", bytes: 120 },
    { id: "utils", parent: "viz", name: "utils", bytes: 80 },
    { id: "core", parent: "src", name: "core" },
    { id: "table", parent: "core", name: "DataTable", bytes: 96 },
    { id: "shell", parent: "core", name: "UIShell", bytes: 74 },
    { id: "rest", parent: "core", name: "everything else", bytes: 60 },
    { id: "vendor", parent: "bundle", name: "vendor" },
    { id: "svelte", parent: "vendor", name: "svelte", bytes: 42 },
    { id: "flatpickr", parent: "vendor", name: "flatpickr", bytes: 58 },
    { id: "assets", parent: "bundle", name: "assets" },
    { id: "css", parent: "assets", name: "css", bytes: 140 },
    { id: "fonts", parent: "assets", name: "fonts", bytes: 90 },
  ];
  const kb = { style: "unit", unit: "kilobyte", unitDisplay: "narrow" };

  let root = null;
  let selected = null;
</script>

`IcicleChart` is part of the data visualization subpackage. Import it from `carbon-components-svelte/viz` and load `carbon-components-svelte/css/viz.css` alongside `all.css`. With a single-theme stylesheet such as `g100.css`, load the matching `viz-g100.css` instead. The subpackage is experimental: its API may change in a minor release.

It takes the same rows as [TreeChart](/components/TreeChart), each with an `id` and a `parent`, and lays them out as a partition: every level is a row, every node is as wide as its share of the root, and children sit directly under their parent. Where a [TreemapChart](/components/TreemapChart) shows two levels well, an icicle shows any depth and keeps the levels legible. [FlameGraph](/components/FlameGraph) is the same chart with the root at the bottom.

## Basic

Pass long `data` with `id`, `parent`, and `value` accessors. A node with no parent is a root. A parent without a value is worth its children, and never less than they add up to. Nodes take the color of their branch, the ancestor just under the root, so each branch has a hue.

<IcicleChart data={bundle} id="id" parent="parent" value="bytes" label="name" format={kb} title="Bundle composition" />

## Order and depth

Children keep their input order. Set `sort` to `"value"` to put the largest first, `maxDepth` to stop after that many levels, and `rowHeight` for the height of a level.

<IcicleChart data={bundle} id="id" parent="parent" value="bytes" label="name" format={kb} sort="value" maxDepth={2} rowHeight={36} title="Bundle composition, two levels, largest first" />

## Drill

Bind `root` to the node to show from. Its ancestors stay above it, dimmed and at full width, as steps back up. The example below sets `root` from `select`, and a step back sets it to the ancestor.

<IcicleChart data={bundle} id="id" parent="parent" value="bytes" label="name" format={kb} selectable bind:root title="Bundle composition, click to drill" on:select={(e) => (root = e.detail.node.id)} />

## Values and colors

Set `valueType` to `"percent"` to write shares instead of values, or `"none"` to write only labels. Set `group` to color by a field of your own, `palette` to pick another color group, and `colors` to fix a color per group.

<IcicleChart data={bundle} id="id" parent="parent" value="bytes" label="name" valueType="percent" palette={2} colors={{ vendor: "neutral" }} title="Bundle composition, as shares" />

## Selectable

Set `selectable` to make each node a toggle button. Bind `selected` or listen for `select`, whose detail carries the node and its `datum`.

<IcicleChart data={bundle} id="id" parent="parent" value="bytes" label="name" format={kb} selectable bind:selected title="Pick a node" />

## Accessibility

Slices are hidden from assistive technology, which reads the same nodes as a table with their level, value, and share, captioned with the title. A slice too narrow for its text keeps the text for the tooltip and the table. When `selectable`, the slices are toggle buttons instead, each named with its label and value, with arrow key navigation in reading order and a single tab stop. Text sits on a chip of the page background, so it stays readable over any branch color in any theme.
